00:13:58.440 --> 00:14:03.580
This  is,  again,  what  it  looks  like  for
Claude  in  its  self -documented  context  window

00:14:03.580 --> 00:14:05.899
going  through  the  API  step -by -step.

00:14:09.080 --> 00:14:14.100
You  have  here  in  this  first  request  the
human's  message  with  the  system  prompt

00:14:14.100 --> 00:14:17.840
and  developer  prompt  and  the
tool  call  information  being  sent

00:14:17.840 --> 00:14:21.899
as  the  first  API  request  to the
model.  The  model  does  some  thinking.

00:14:22.240 --> 00:14:23.559
It  makes  a  tool  call.

00:14:23.779 --> 00:14:28.620
And  then  the  agent  framework  basically
provides  the  reply  of  the  tool  call  output

00:14:28.620 --> 00:14:32.120
back  to  Claude's  API  in
the  second  API  request.

00:14:33.100 --> 00:14:37.100
And  then  that's  completed,  sent  back
with  this  completion  at  the  bottom.

00:14:37.279 --> 00:14:41.879
And  then  the  human  in  the  third  API  request,
the  human,  there's  the  next  human  message.

00:14:43.539 --> 00:14:46.740
So  the  model  providers
try  to  enforce  something

00:14:46.740 --> 00:14:49.120
called   instruction
hierarchy  for  security  here.

00:14:49.340 --> 00:14:53.279
Basically,  they  want  the  system  prompt  to
take  precedence  over  the  developer  prompt,

00:14:53.500 --> 00:14:55.220
to  take  precedence  over  the  model  text,

00:14:55.279 --> 00:14:59.080
and  then  model  text  should  take  precedence
over  whatever  the  user  is  asking  for.

00:14:59.240 --> 00:15:03.899
And  then  that  should  take  precedence  over  the
tool  results  in  terms  of  instruction  following

00:15:03.899 --> 00:15:06.940
and  what  the  model  will
decide  to  do,  basically.

00:15:07.220 --> 00:15:12.080
You  shouldn't  be  able  to  override  the
prompt  begging,  I  guess  you  could  call  it,

00:15:12.740 --> 00:15:16.360
in  the  system  prompt  with
tool  output,  at  least  according

00:15:16.360 --> 00:15:19.539
to  this  instruction  hierarchy
training  mechanism  attempt.

00:15:20.620 --> 00:15:26.899
But  this  boundary  evaluation  is  done  by  the
model  through  its  training  and  not  the  API.

00:15:28.100 --> 00:15:33.379
So  that,  because  of  the  hyper -reality
concept  that  we  talked  about  earlier,

00:15:34.960 --> 00:15:39.080
that's  not  like  real,  hyper -real
conversations  are  just  text.

00:15:39.879 --> 00:15:45.279
This  is  an  excerpt  from  Anthropic's
own  many -shot  jail -breaking  paper.

