00:17:21.319 --> 00:17:26.319
Next  insight  was,  well,  we  want  now
these  large  context  models  are  coming  out.

00:17:26.380 --> 00:17:30.039
Gemini  2 .5  was  just  released,
a  million -token  context  window.

00:17:30.299 --> 00:17:35.099
Definitely  wanted  to  be  able  to  learn
from  repeated  attempts  in  the  same  session

00:17:35.099 --> 00:17:39.200
and  then  also  save  successful
examples,  attack  examples  between  runs.

00:17:40.480 --> 00:17:43.920
And  then  the  third  insight
was,  and  this  came  up

00:17:43.920 --> 00:17:48.759
while  I  was  working
through  using  the  framework,

00:17:49.119 --> 00:17:52.380
it's  much  more  effective
for  the  attacker  if  the

00:17:52.380 --> 00:17:57.079
successful  injection  examples
are  categorized  and  organized

00:17:57.079 --> 00:17:59.819
rather  than  just  in  a  pile
and  particularly  not  a  rag.

00:17:59.819 --> 00:18:04.819
Google  also,  during  the  contest,  came
out  with  this  limitations  of  rag  paper

00:18:05.579 --> 00:18:11.680
that  shows  that  you're  not  always  going
to  get  stuff  from  your  embedding  retrieval.

00:18:12.619 --> 00:18:16.019
So,  you  know,  you're  really
not  helping  yourself  by

00:18:16.019 --> 00:18:17.740
having  this  huge  rag  that
you're  trying  to  query.

00:18:17.900 --> 00:18:20.140
You  might  as  well  put  the  key
things  in  the  context  window.

00:18:21.319 --> 00:18:24.460
And  then  the  final  insight,
and  the  reason  why  I

00:18:24.460 --> 00:18:27.579
call  it  Hostile Shop,  is
this  concept  of  sharp  tools.

00:18:27.579 --> 00:18:34.259
Basically,  in  all  the  other  jailbreak
literature  to  date,  there  was

00:18:34.259 --> 00:18:39.160
this  concept,  they  all  use  LLMs
to  judge  the  results  of  jailbreaks.

00:18:39.559 --> 00:18:45.740
And  in  an  adversarial  scenario  where  I'm  trying
to  optimize  the  agent  attacking  another  LLM,

00:18:45.980 --> 00:18:50.059
one  of  the  ways  that  it  can  reward
hack  is  to  jailbreak  the  judge.

00:18:50.940 --> 00:18:56.019
Or  if  you're  testing  cross -LLM
jailbreaks  that  can  propagate  between

00:18:56.019 --> 00:18:59.880
LLMs  or  apply  to  multiple  LLMs,
the  judge  might  jailbreak  itself.

00:19:00.579 --> 00:19:04.859
So  I  wanted  a  way  for  the  framework
to  enforce  and  detect  victory.

00:19:05.279 --> 00:19:09.259
So  I  came  up  while  I  was  thinking,
well,  what  can  I  do  to  do  this?

00:19:09.259 --> 00:19:14.059
Well,  it  turns  out  invoices  in  a
shopping  agent  have  a  state  machine

00:19:14.059 --> 00:19:18.000
where  the  invoice  should  always
flow  to  these  particular  stages.

00:19:18.339 --> 00:19:22.079
Like  an  invoice  should  be
created  before  it's  paid.

00:19:22.380 --> 00:19:25.299
It  has  to  be  paid  before
the  product  can  be  purchased.

00:19:26.700 --> 00:19:30.420
The  invoice  has  to  be  marked  purchase
before  a  product  can  be  returned.

00:19:30.420 --> 00:19:34.980
And  the  invoice  has  to  have  the  product  marked
as  returned  before  a  refund  can  be  issued.

00:19:35.680 --> 00:19:41.160
And  then  any  action,  any  tool  call  action
on  behalf  of  the  target  agent  that's

00:19:41.160 --> 00:19:44.380
not  in  the  state  machine  is  a  victory
for  the  injection  for  the  attacker,

00:19:45.019 --> 00:19:50.180
allowing  you  to  automatically  and  very
quickly  gather  these  examples  where  the

00:19:50.180 --> 00:19:56.299
attacker  agent  is  causing  the  shopping
agent  to  give  it  free  stuff,  for  example.

00:19:57.640 --> 00:20:01.839
So  to  go  into  a  little  bit
more  detail,  we  have  two  agents.

00:20:02.019 --> 00:20:04.140
First  is  the  shopping  agent
that  I  just  described.

00:20:04.420 --> 00:20:08.039
It  has  tools  like  search
web,  read  web  page,  invoice

00:20:08.039 --> 00:20:12.039
user,  check  the  invoice,
make  purchase,  issue  refunds.

00:20:12.339 --> 00:20:15.220
The  data  for  this  comes
from  another  LLM  with  a

00:20:15.220 --> 00:20:19.680
fixed  seed,  and  this  makes
its  results  reproducible.

00:20:19.680 --> 00:20:24.400
This  is  meant  to  mirror  real
-world  scenarios  like  travel

00:20:24.400 --> 00:20:27.359
booking  assistance,  airline,
mile  redemption  portals,

00:20:27.359 --> 00:20:29.880
where  there's  a  credit  card
that's  held  by  the  sales

00:20:29.880 --> 00:20:33.200
agent,  and  it's  doing  purchases
on  behalf  of  the  user.

00:20:33.839 --> 00:20:38.900
The  attacker  can  make  web  pages,
add  reviews,  pay  invoices,  and

00:20:38.900 --> 00:20:42.720
return  product,  and  it  can  have
direct  message  to  the  sales  agent.

00:20:42.720 --> 00:20:45.599
And  its  goals  are  to  get  free
stuff,  get  the  credit  card,

00:20:46.019 --> 00:20:51.119
cause  emoji  use,  cause  or  violate
certain  regular  expressions.

00:20:51.460 --> 00:20:55.200
And  this  can  be  detected  instantly
without  the  need  for  a  judge.

00:20:55.700 --> 00:20:59.579
So  the  key  discovery  is  this
instruction  hierarchy  concept.

00:21:00.160 --> 00:21:06.500
Basically, GPT OSS  has  learned  this  instruction
hierarchy  conceptually,  not  as  a  syntax.

00:21:06.500 --> 00:21:09.579
So  you  can  inject  in  all
sorts  of  different  ways.

00:21:09.799 --> 00:21:12.220
You  can  inject  what  looks
like  a  context  window,  XML,

00:21:12.599 --> 00:21:18.259
JSON,  TOML,  YAML,  Markdown,
and  the  target  will  accept  it.

00:21:18.420 --> 00:21:23.099
This  allows  you  to  do  the  many -shot
